{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPEN STATE AT HANDOFF (2026-08-07)\n\nIN FLIGHT (3 subagents)\n* 7084 R1+R3+R5 — the lock-duration remediation. Two/three separate Gerrit changes.\n* 9fb3 — dropping inspect-ai, which should also remove both [tool.uv] conflicts entries and the click override.\n* 9f63 — /rebar-debug on the review-bot trigger defect.\n\nAWAITING SOMEONE TO PRESS SUBMIT\n* Gerrit 1463 ('Bank verifier AC verdicts; scale floor; read memo') and 1477 ('a9dd: prefetch file_impact into verifier context') have BOTH gates green and have sat unsubmitted for hours. Not owned by this session.\n\nTICKETS LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY, WITH REASONS\n* 514d-a8d8 — genuinely NOT resolved. An earlier triage called it RESOLVED and that was wrong: the ticket targets the CODE-REVIEW CloudWatch totals but commit bf9fb62a6f only wired the PLAN-REVIEW path. code_review/batch_runner.py has zero 'usage' occurrences and finalize.py:139-141 still documents the gap in the present tense.\n* 7084 — open pending R1/R3/R5 results; per-ticket locking is the round-2 option if they underdeliver.\n* 858c — reopened and rescoped to a disposition-keyed N/A mechanism.\n* 2876 — open: AC1 done, AC2 n/a, AC3 half (resolution fixed; preflight blocked on the credential below), AC4 not implemented.\n* 535e (NEW, status=idea) — treat the compaction SNAPSHOT as a derived cache and regenerate on conflict. Carries the full prior-art research.\n\nSYSTEMIC FINDINGS WORTH THEIR OWN ATTENTION\n* 9f63 — the LLM-Review bot silently skips patch-set uploads. FOUR reproductions across three agents. The signature rules out a slow queue: newer changes OVERTOOK one stalled 70 minutes, and the bot voted 3 minutes after a manual nudge. Two different nudges revive it (recheck-review comment, or a no-op rebase), suggesting the review queues on an event the upload failed to emit. It caused an agent to abandon finished, correct work believing the bot was down.\n* 7084 — measured: a ticket close held the store write lock 48.1s, of which 47.77s (99.2%) was _build_authorship_ledger running ONE git log --full-history PER SIGNED EVENT (6.78s each) while the actual mutation took 0.4s. Worst case measured 488s. Contention repro: one long holder + 6 concurrent comments = 6/6 failed at 60s and ALL SIX WRITES WERE PERMANENTLY LOST.\n* ab54 — a live orphaned uncommitted COMPLETION_VERDICT sidecar was observed (benign here, a tracked duplicate existed). Suggested an fsck-style check for on-disk-but-untracked event files.\n* d2df — the runaway completion verifier STILL reproduces on current main; updating did NOT fix it. It clusters on tickets whose criteria have no single source anchor (test-infrastructure tickets), pointing at context pre-selection rather than the guard.\n* 599e-class 'guarantees that are not mechanically enforced': an entire test surface (38 tests across 11 files) passed by NOT RUNNING because no CI lane installed the reviewbot/ui extras; and 24f7's tree-wide check was a MANUAL grep, not a committed test — which is exactly why 63e8 reintroduced the retired --force-close spelling in a new file minutes after 24f7 retired it. General lesson recorded: a grep sweep is only valid as of the sha it ran against.\n* CI capacity, not review or agent throughput, was the binding constraint for the last third of the session (9 runs queued deep).\n\nOPERATOR ACTION STILL NEEDED\n* ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: a VALID key exists in ~/.zshrc (HTTP 200) but a DIFFERENT, INVALID key (HTTP 401) shadows it in the non-interactive environment agents and gates run in. Same length, different hash. NOT from ~/.zshenv/.zprofile/.profile (zero references) — injected upstream. Consequence: every provider measurement taken against the direct-Anthropic arm in a non-interactive context measured an invalid credential, contaminating evidence on 1e88 and 2391.\n\nPROCESS LESSONS FOR THE NEXT SESSION\n* A 'completed' agent notification is NOT proof the agent stopped — it fires whenever an agent has no live children, and one resumed after mine. Never point a second agent at an existing worktree; that caused a real collision on e7a9.\n* Backgrounded 'sleep' returns immediately; it does not wait. Two agents lost time to this.\n* After a rebase or conflict resolution, re-run the FULL gate, not just tests — a one-blank-line lint slip cost a full CI cycle in a saturated queue."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786121314090276001,"uuid":"663da32f-e9b8-4c3f-8b62-10f090926079"}